# agents/BOOTSTRAP_SHA — supply-chain pin for the bootstrap repo (P1-B)
#
# ══ ROTATION IS MANDATORY ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
#   Since 2026-09-16 this is a REAL pin: the runner checks the commit below out
#   DETACHED and runs it. Master moving does NOT reach the fleet. Nothing you
#   push to grotap-agents — a lesson file, a runner fix, a task — is read by any
#   agent until the SHA below names a commit that contains it.
#   Rotate it as part of the same piece of work, not "later".  Steps: §ROTATION.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# WHAT THIS PINS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A DOCS PROBLEM
#   This repo. `agents/scripts/orchestrator-run.sh` — the runner the LangGraph
#   orchestrator SSHes into on every fleet host — refreshes ~/grotap-agents from
#   origin on every single run, and the NEXT run executes this repo's copy of
#   that script. Whatever tree ends up on disk therefore runs as the `agent`
#   user, on every box, one run later. That is remote code execution on the
#   fleet, gated by nothing but push access, and until this file existed it was
#   gated by nothing at all. (`agents/dispatch.sh` in grotap-platform
#   additionally cats BOOTSTRAP.md and agents/GLOBAL.md from here straight into
#   the model prompt.)
#
# WHO READS IT
#   grotap-agents : agents/scripts/orchestrator-run.sh -> verify_bootstrap_pin()
#                   + checkout_bootstrap_pin() — the LIVE path, invoked as
#                   `bash ~/grotap-agents/agents/scripts/orchestrator-run.sh`
#                   (orchestrator/src/nodes/execute.ts, deploy.ts, ds-steps.ts).
#   grotap-platform : agents/scripts/orchestrator-run.sh (section 2b), which
#                   keeps its own copy of this file and does the same thing.
#                   Two repos, two copies, on purpose — each verifies the side
#                   of the trust boundary it actually crosses. Rotate BOTH.
#                   agents/dispatch.sh deliberately carries NO pin check of its
#                   own; the control lives on the runner path (see the comment
#                   at dispatch.sh "Bootstrap tree integrity (P1-B)").
#   SECURITY_OPERATIONS.md 2.1 [MUST] / 2.3 smoke check (grotap-platform).
#
# FORMAT
#   Comments start with '#'. Exactly one line is a bare 40-character lowercase
#   hex commit SHA; the reader takes the first such line (CR is stripped, so a
#   Windows-edited file still parses). A file with no such line is UNPINNED with
#   a loud warning on a host that has never verified a pin — deliberately not a
#   hard failure, because this file reaches hosts through the very self-sync it
#   guards — and a HARD FAILURE on a host that has verified one before
#   ($HOME/.grotap_bootstrap_pin_seen, which lives outside the git tree so a
#   push cannot clear it).
#
# WHAT THE RUNNER DOES WITH IT — the modes of ORCH_BOOTSTRAP_PIN
#
#   detach   DEFAULT ('on' and 'pin' are aliases; an unrecognised value is
#            treated as this one, with a warning, so a typo cannot disable the
#            control). The pinned commit must EXIST in the fetched repo; the
#            runner then `git checkout --force --detach <pin>` and CONFIRMS
#            HEAD equals it, aborting if it does not. What runs is the blessed
#            commit, wherever master has gone.
#            WHY THIS CANNOT BRICK THE FLEET: a stale pin degrades to "agents
#            read a slightly older BOOTSTRAP.md", never to "no agents run". The
#            check is "the pinned commit exists", which a routine push cannot
#            falsify — unlike the exact-match pin that was ripped out in
#            2026-09, which asserted "master tip == this hardcoded SHA" and so
#            failed on every box at once after any push, burning a retry strike
#            per case because nothing caps dispatch strikes.
#            DOES NOT CATCH: a malicious commit that someone with push access
#            got BLESSED by rotating this file. Reading the diff at rotation
#            time is the control; step 2 below is not a formality.
#            FORCE-PUSH / REWRITE: still detected (the pin stops being an
#            ancestor of the tip) and logged as `SECURITY WARNING: pinned <sha>
#            is NOT an ancestor of <tip>`, but it does NOT abort — the tree that
#            runs is the blessed one either way, and aborting there would hand
#            anyone with push access a fleet-wide kill switch.
#
#   ancestor The pre-2026-09-16 DETECTOR, kept as a one-env-var rollback: the
#            tip must BE the pin or descend from it, and then the TIP is what
#            runs. Catches a rewrite; does not control what executes.
#
#   exact    The fetched tip must EQUAL the pin. Real containment, and it stops
#            the fleet the moment grotap-agents master moves. A lockdown window,
#            never a steady state. How fast does that bite? Measured 2026-09-15
#            inside one working session, master went 79b23e3f -> e87c79d2 ->
#            c24423e8. Three tips in one afternoon.
#
#   off      (also 0/false/no) No check, loud warning in the run log, per host,
#            next dispatch. Emergency bypass while you rotate properly.
#
# ROTATION
#   1. Resolve the tip from the REMOTE, never from a local checkout (a local
#      clone can be stale or peer-dirty):
#        git ls-remote https://github.com/Grotap-AI/grotap-agents.git refs/heads/master
#   2. Read what you are about to bless. This is the point of the pin:
#        git log --oneline <old>..<new>
#        git diff <old>..<new> -- agents/scripts/orchestrator-run.sh \
#                                 BOOTSTRAP.md agents/GLOBAL.md
#      The runner script is executed; those two markdown files become model
#      instructions. Anything touching them gets read, not skimmed.
#   3. Replace the SHA line below, commit, push to master.
#   4. Mirror the SAME SHA into grotap-platform's agents/BOOTSTRAP_SHA and push
#      that too, or the two runner copies disagree about what is blessed.
#   5. WHEN THE CHANGE BEING SHIPPED IS TO THIS REPO, ROTATE TWICE. The commit
#      that records a SHA cannot contain its own SHA, so the rotation commit is
#      itself unblessed: hosts alternate between the pinned tree and the tip
#      until a FOLLOW-UP commit pins the merge commit. Not a brick — each run is
#      internally consistent — but it means your change is live on only every
#      other run until you push that second rotation.
#   6. Confirm on the next dispatch — the run log should carry one of:
#        Bootstrap tree VERIFIED at pinned <sha> (mode=detach; the pin IS the current tip)
#        Bootstrap tree PINNED at <sha> (mode=detach) — tip <sha> is N commit(s) ahead ...
#        Bootstrap tree CHECKED OUT detached at pinned <sha>
